<section class="page-content wrap">

<p>A literature review isn't just busywork — it's how you prove you actually understand the field before you add to it. The problem is volume: dozens of papers, half of them behind dense jargon, all before you write a single sentence of your own. These tools won't write your review for you (and you shouldn't let them), but they'll cut the research time down dramatically.</p>

<h2>1. Elicit</h2>
<p>Elicit is built specifically for literature reviews. Type in your research question and it searches academic databases, pulls relevant papers, and summarizes each one's key findings in a table you can scan in minutes. It's especially good for spotting which studies actually answer your question versus ones that just mention your topic in passing.</p>

<h2>2. Consensus</h2>
<p>Consensus works like a search engine for scientific claims. Ask it something like "does spaced repetition improve retention?" and it pulls findings from real papers, tells you how many studies agree or disagree, and links straight to the sources. Great for quickly checking whether a claim you want to make is actually backed by evidence.</p>

<h2>3. Scholarcy</h2>
<p>Scholarcy takes a dense PDF and turns it into a structured summary — key findings, methodology, limitations — in seconds. Upload a paper you're not sure is worth a full read, and Scholarcy tells you in 30 seconds whether it's relevant enough to dig into properly.</p>

<h2>4. Scite</h2>
<p>Scite shows you how a paper has been cited by others — and whether those later papers supported or contradicted its findings. This is genuinely useful for literature reviews because it helps you catch outdated or since-disputed research before you build an argument on it.</p>

<h2>5. QuillBot (Summarizer tool)</h2>
<p>Once you've gathered your papers, QuillBot's summarizer can condense long sections into a few sentences to help you keep track of what each source actually argued, especially useful when you're juggling 20+ papers and need a quick refresher before writing.</p>

<h2>A quick note on using these responsibly</h2>
<p>These tools are for finding and understanding sources faster — not for generating your analysis or your citations wholesale. Always verify a paper actually says what a tool claims it says, and check your university's policy on AI-assisted research before you rely on one for graded work.</p>

